      "fragmentText": "03What changes in one operation?Every candidate satisfies i+j=half; a valid cut remains in [lo,hi]. Once both cross comparisons hold, odd length uses max(aL,bL), while even length averages it with min(aR,bR).Keep one input fixed and execute the next operation. Inspect both cuts and all four boundaries; predict the search direction first. The readout shows the updated search range while the cuts retain the just-tested positions; rewind restores all state.\nExperiment input (changing restarts)Odd totalEven total and boundaryOne empty arrayDuplicates and negativesDecrease i: exclude the wrong direction\n[[1,3],[2]]\nRewind one operationExecute next operationRestart this experiment\nEvent positionEvent 0 / 2\nInitial state; no operation has run.\nHighlight and underline mark positions involved in this operation; indices appear below values.Shorter array a20Array b1031\nPrevious stateNo previous frame yet.\nAlgorithm state (variables and diagram indices)lo0hi1half2i∅j∅\n\nCorresponding Python operation (focused excerpt)lo, hi = 0, m\nRecent operations (up to 6 retained)0: Initial state; no operation has run.\n"
    },
    {
      "locale": "en",
      "label": "Continue to #668: find the first sufficient value",
      "href": "http://127.0.0.1:18770/leetcode/668/#step2",
      "fragmentText": "033 · Eliminate half the valuesWhich answers can we exclude using C(x), the number of cells with value ≤x? For 3×3 and k=5, C(2)=3: only three cells are ≤2, so the fifth must exceed 2. But C(3)=5: five cells are already ≤3, so the answer is at most 3.Increasing x never removes a qualifying cell: C is nondecreasing. The answer is therefore the first integer x with C(x)≥k. Requiring C(x)=k fails: in the same 3×3 table with k=4, C(2)=3 and C(3)=5. The count skips 4, yet the fourth cell is still 3.Let lo and hi bound an inclusive interval containing the answer, and mid=⌊(lo+hi)/2⌋. If C(mid)<k, mid and every smaller value are insufficient: set lo=mid+1. If C(mid)≥k, mid could be the answer: set hi=mid, rather than mid−1. The default first round tests mid=5 in [1,9]; C(5)=3+2+1=6, leaving [1,5]. The next round tests 3; C(3)=5, leaving [1,3]. This halving of the candidate value range is binary search on the answer.These observations give an invariant, a fact kept after every round: C(lo−1)<k≤C(hi). Every value left of lo is insufficient, while hi is sufficient, so the first sufficient value remains in [lo,hi]. Initially lo=1 and hi=m×n, since C(0)=0 and C(m×n)=m×n.This is a JavaScript teaching simulation, not execution of the Python submission in the browser. Each frame completes one full count and interval update; the initial frame has not counted yet. Cell marks refer to the just-tested mid, while the updated interval is labeled separately. No rounds are omitted.Keep a preset fixed and choose “Complete one count round.” Compare the old interval with the new one, then compare retained ranges in the history. “Back one round” restores every value. Changing the preset restarts this experiment; the threshold slider and prediction stay unchanged.Preset (changing restarts)3×3 · k="
    },
